The reaction to our work was not uniformly positive.
 In particular, our focus on biases was criticized as suggesting an unfairly negative view of the mind.
 As expected in normal science, some investigators refined our ideas and others offered plausible alternatives.
 By and large, though, the idea that our minds are susceptible to systematic errors is now generally accepted.
 Our research on judgment had far more effect on social science than we thought possible when we were working on it.
